the freshly picked taro. "We don't grow much sugarcane on the

29
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:30.327
islands anymore," Grandma said. "But my family, like many of the first Asian immigrants to Hawaii, came to work on sugarcane plantations

30
00:02:30.328 --> 00:02:36.965
in the 1800s." Kaycee pulled the tough skin off a steamed taro as they started to

31
00:02:36.966 --> 00:02:39.041
prepare the poi.

32
00:02:39.042 --> 00:02:43.671
"What's poi anyway?" "It's mashed taro mixed with

33
00:02:43.672 --> 00:02:47.476
water," Grandma explained. "It's a staple food here

34
00:02:47.477 --> 00:02:53.672
on the islands." Kaycee pounded the roots with a stone pestle as

35
00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:57.684
Grandma added water. Kaycee looked doubtfully at the smooth,

36
00:02:57.685 --> 00:03:00.382
purplish gray paste. "It's

37
00:03:00.383 --> 00:03:03.403
good!" Mom promised. She dipped two fingers in

38
00:03:03.404 --> 00:03:09.597
and tasted it. Kaycee tasted it just like Mom had. "Wow, it

39
00:03:09.598 --> 00:03:14.805
is delicious!" That evening carloads of guests arrived, and people swarmed to welcome Kaycee and

40
00:03:14.806 --> 00:03:19.826
her mother. Ohana was not just relatives, Kaycee realized. It extended

41
00:03:19.827 --> 00:03:22.567
to friends. Every guest had brought a

42
